    {
      "id": "LR006",
      "section": "Sequential Logic (Registers)",
      "start_line": 1818,
      "end_line": 1820,
      "text": "Sequential statements for state assignments should only contain reset values and\na next-state to state assignment, use a separate combinational-only block to\ngenerate that next-state value.",
      "sha256": "e21796acf251a073061ee4e103ad61404d82cbafca689dc2df125e2c0df8e647",
      "characters": 189,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    },
    {
      "id": "LR015",
      "section": "Case Statements",
      "start_line": 2189,
      "end_line": 2192,
      "text": "  - a `default:` statement is **always** included in order to avoid accidental\n  inference of latches, even if all cases are covered. In simulation, a case\n  expression that evaluates to `X` will not match any case and will behave as a\n  latch, leading to different behavior than synthesis if no default is specified.",
      "sha256": "671cab8965910cd22aa4aa60fb141c20ee77102e6da34dd8c28e743cf5ec15e0",
      "characters": 317,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    },
    {
      "id": "LR018",
      "section": "Case Statements",
      "start_line": 2245,
      "end_line": 2252,
      "text": "`casex` should not be used. `casex` implements a symmetric wildcard operator\nsuch that an `X` in the case expression may match one or more case items.\n`casez` only treats high-impedance states (`Z` or `?`) as a wildcard, and\nperforms exact matches for undriven `X` inputs. While this does not completely\nfix the problems with symmetric wildcard matching, it is harder to accidentally\nproduce a `Z` input than an `X` input, so this form is preferred.\n`case inside` does not treat either `X` or `Z` in the case expression as a\nwildcard, so this form is preferred over `casez`.",
      "sha256": "38b511f542f8fcb62c5ad046b40e95a8c569d5e5b69bdce9dbe2cc6e393640e9",
      "characters": 574,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    }
  ],
